One workaround that I found is to extract the permission check into a separate hook, and then if you want to remove that permission then you just patch that hook in one place. This is obviously suboptimal, but it achieves the purpose. Example:

{noformat}import * as React from "react";

import {usePermissionsApi} from "@admin-panel/common/hooks/ModuleApiProvider";

export function useCanEditComponents() {
    const permissionsApi = usePermissionsApi();

    const canEdit = React.useMemo(
        // here you can add "&& false" or "|| true" for local testing, the linter won't complain about it
        () => permissionsApi.hasPermission("component_management_edit"),
        [permissionsApi],
    );

    return canEdit;
}
{noformat}

Please note that this concerns only frontend permission check, as the backend check is done completely independently on backend and would require a different solution.

{quote}The fact that individual items of the data are publicly available does not exclude the risk of IP infringement. The risk arises primarily due to a set of data (not public as a whole) obtained as a result of long-term automated data collection. The court practice in data scraping disputes is inconsistent, but there have been cases where courts have found that the fact that the data is taken from a publicly accessible website does not mean its scraping is legal.{quote}

I might be incorrect, if so please correct. Lets ensure the contract mitigates this risk above. The services requested should not detail how the 3rd party to is to acquire the data, only that on a bi-weekly basis they furnish us with a csv. with only *publicly available data,* we should also specify that they remove/omit any non-publicly available information. They should omit data where they have to expressly accept a website T&Cs, i.e. while we shouldn’t write this, effectively they should not be logged in with an account.

We may want to explicitly state they must comply with robots.txt this is the web scraping/crawling directives from the domain/source. It further mitigates risk by being compliant with this. However by explicitly stating it we are admitting knowledge and we are directing how they acquire the data. I would recommend staying away from this, maybe just bundling it together in a catch all “they represent and warrant that the source of all data sent through to Bolt will be compliant with any ToS and robots.txt and derived only from publicly available sources” (obviously worded better). They should accept this as risk for them is even lower.

We should also be explicit about the data we require. Restaurant phone numbers are not owned by google or other food tech platforms. (Unless they use redirect numbers which is not common practice).
Restaurant addresses likewise. The grey area could be on any additional data such as reviews / ratings which are behind a ToS wall (login). For these ones we should be very clear the deliverable must not be in breach of any ToS, and ensure the reviews/ratings are publicly available.

{quote}* Scale and frequency on an actual need basis:
** Need-based: e.g. when forming, reviewing, changing our pricing strategy.
** Reactive: e.g. when competitor adjust their pricing strategy;
** Periodical: what is the reasoning for the biweekly reporting frequency? Why not monthly?{quote}

Bi-weekly minimum. TLs assign leads daily, under the new defined sales functions monthly would mean we would need to change our sales functions again and probably remove the performance sales function all together. Ideally we want weekly and should build towards this. We run a global sales function, the need for sales leads is a daily business requirement.

{quote}* Periodic deletion of data. In addition to limiting the quantity of data that will be scraped, it is also important to limit the quantity of data that will be kept. It may be difficult to escape from liability and, from the other side, it is more easier for competitors to prove that their database was copied, if all data is kept and never deleted.{quote}

We will still have digital footprint. I think having a defined process to delete the source files will not help in court when we have a contract with a 3rd party in place. We could put an expiry, source files are purged after 12 months?

{quote}* Ownership of data vs license to use. Do we need to own the data or is it sufficient if it is stored by a third party service provider and we have an access? Using third party services inserts a distance between the data and Bolt. Competitors have an additional burden of proof to attribute infringing activity to Bolt. The license may also serve as a mechanism to transfer risk to a third party - pricing service with a risk-premium in return for not accepting liability shift.{quote}

This is excellent advice, bang on and congrats for coming up with it. It might be too late to administer this in the current negotiations - if so lets redo the contract in 12 months and only license the data. If we can put it in now, it shouldn’t change the mechanism of anything in actuality, it would just be the ‘framing’ of the contract. We would need to have clear terms for termination of contract i.e. they grant us an irrevocable and unrestricted license to any data previously received etc etc..

