[~accountid:712020:a7a7d4bc-10ca-44a0-a22e-fa8752330abd], thank you for the detailed response.

The key culprit here is definitions: what is a traditional fleet?

* Is it only those who hire drivers? We don’t have/collect this information and cannot reliably validate it;
* Is it only those who are getting funds on a company account?
Then we have financing & rental fleets that collect all the funds to ensure their pay is covered first, and drivers agree on those conditions.

Practically, we want to give extended access to the fleets that are involved in the drivers' engagement on the platform:

# it could be a *hiring* model where complete access is safe, but we don’t have a reliable source of the model and relations with every driver;
# it might be a *rental* fleet that picks the best drivers to perform on the platform to gain a kick-back bonus from us and share the revenue with drivers directly or as a rental fee discount;
# it might even be an *onboarding* fleet that must ensure drivers are not present in their fleet yet (based on their national personal code) or have the same payment credentials as a returning driver;
# Any fleet might want to check a driver’s photo to ensure its owner is not the namesake of a different driver.

Fleets might need such information for various reasons. Still, as long as drivers are willing to share this data, I’d take any reasonable step to ensure self-service for everyone rather than assessing fleets individually ([one approved example|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C0576CT9MQD/p1712049745604419?thread_ts=1711610814.284899&cid=C0576CT9MQD] link vs many fleets missing the tooling).

We might gather the motivations when such access is reasonable and provide a fleet setting that local operation teams could configure.
Then it’s as simple as any T&C: either a driver accepts it (we could provide a justification), or they cannot join a fleet.

AWS engineer reproduced our issue and raised linked issue in kubernetes-sigs:

{quote}[https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/karpenter/issues/1239|https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/karpenter/issues/1239|smart-link] 

The issue laid out in [+aws/karpenter-provider-aws#6141+|https://github.com/aws/karpenter-provider-aws/issues/6141] is a little bit different in terms of symptoms (unable to provision), but the same zonal injection behavior is to blame. In that issue there were 5 pods in the StatefulSet, each with zonal and hostname anti-affinity. When the StatefulSet was scaled to 3 pods and back to 5, Karpenter failed to provision additional nodes believing TSC would be violated. Since Karpenter is only counting nodes in the same zone as the volume when computing skew, and it is aware of all zones, it believes it must create a node in a different zone to fulfill the TSC requirement. Since this is not possible, we fail to provision.{quote}

