*3.4 Cláusula Compliance Penal*
3.4.1 O Cliente dispõe de um Modelo de Prevenção Penal cujo objetivo é prevenir e evitar a prática de crimes no seio da organização, nos termos do disposto no Código Penal e legislação conexa quanto à responsabilidade penal das pessoas coletivas.
3.4.2 Por este motivo, o Cliente reforçou o seu modelo de organização, prevenção, gestão e controlo, o qual foi desenhado em total consonância com a sua cultura de cumprimento.
3.4.3 O Cliente dispõe de uma política de compliance penal, {color:#bf2600}de que disponibilizou uma cópia à Bolt{color} e cujos termos e condições este último este declara conhecer, na qual se estabelecem os seguintes princípios gerais que devem ser cumpridos igualmente pelos seus prestadores de serviços, naquilo que esteja em conformidade com as políticas internas da Bolt e relativas aos seus fornecedores:
a) Atuar em conformidade com a legislação em vigor a todo o momento;
b) Promover uma cultura corporativa de prevenção e de intolerância para com a prática de atos ilícitos ou fraudulentos, assim como fomentar a aplicação dos princípios éticos e de comportamentos responsáveis;
c) Garantir a existência de sistemas de controlo eficazes, permanentes e atualizados a todo o momento;
d) Supervisionar a submissão de todas as ações e decisões adotadas no Cliente às normas e procedimentos internos estabelecidos, bem como aos controlos correspondentes;
e) Assegurar a existência dos recursos e meios adequados para a aplicação da referida política de compliance penal a fim de prevenir ou detetar eventuais práticas de crimes;
f) Realizar atividades de formação que se revelem adequadas e necessárias, com periodicidade suficiente, para assegurar a atualização dos conhecimentos nesta matéria e o desenvolvimento de uma cultura de ética empresarial e de cumprimento da lei;
g) Transmitir a todas as pessoas singulares ou coletivas enquadradas no âmbito de aplicação da mencionada política de compliance penal, a sua responsabilidade pela vigilância de comportamentos potencialmente ilícitos numa perspetiva penal;
h) Informar todos os sujeitos enquadrados no âmbito da política de compliance penal acerca da obrigação de reportar, através dos meios definidos em cada momento, sobre qualquer facto potencialmente constitutivo de crime, fraude ou irregularidade de que tenham tomado conhecimento;
i) Promover a todo o momento uma cultura de cumprimento que, com as devidas garantias de confidencialidade e proteção do denunciante, favoreça e incentive a comunicação de possíveis riscos e irregularidades de natureza penal;
j) Investigar, com a maior brevidade os factos, presumivelmente ilícitos, garantindo, em qualquer caso, os direitos das pessoas envolvidas na investigação;
k) Zelar pela divulgação das sanções disciplinares previstas, de acordo com a Política interna e as disposições legais vigentes.

*3.5 Cláusula Código Ético e Princípios de Atuação*
3.5.1 O Cliente dispõe de um Código Ético e Princípios de Atuação, disponível [www.bancobpi.pt|http://www.bancobpi.pt], que estabelece os valores (qualidade, confiança e compromisso social) e os princípios éticos (Cumprimento das Leis, o Respeito, a Integridade, a Transparência, a Excelência, o Profissionalismo, a Confidencialidade, a Responsabilidade Social) que regem a sua atividade.
3.5.2 Por força da celebração do presente Acordo, a Bolt garante que está alinhada com os referidos valores e princípios, promovendo uma cultura organizacional assente em padrões de ética equiparáveis aos do próprio Cliente.

Good morning Galina,

Sorry for the delayed response, I’ve been busy with training. I think some of my points were not clear, I’ll {color:#006644}try to explain better:{color}

Thank you for your query. Let me address the questions point by point:

{{When selecting the EC, the Cognito Tool tells agents to "PLACE TEMPORARY BLOCK ON ACCUSED PARTY." This implies the Investigation block, but can be confused with other scheduled short-term blocks, and in various places in the KB "temporary block" and "scheduled block" are used for both the investigation block and for scheduled consequential blocks, without sufficient clarity to be sure which is meant.}}

We cannot place consequential blocks without investigating the case in the first place, so the Temporary block in this case means that we need to start investigating the matter.

* {color:#006644}of course, we understand that, but it is ambiguous, and ambiguity leads to confusion during training and more mistakes, especially by new agents. When it comes to use of the term “temporary” in KB, it is not always so clear by context as it is in the Tool.{color}

{{Perhaps it would be possible to add a note in the "Action Required" section of the Tool to remove the Investigation Block after applying the consequential actions.}}

Currently, this part of the KB mentions that we need to act according to the SOP outcome, so if we block someone for 3 days, we would need to remove the infinite investigation block (otherwise, the user will remain blocked infinitely once the 3-day block passes) -

“Based on the information provided by the SOP, inform the user of the outcome (if and for how long their account will be suspended) suggested by the Assessment Tool. Educate them on how to prevent it in the future - only do this where appropriate. If the user is hard-blocked, do not give guidelines on how to prevent it, just inform the user of the block and outline the allegations.”

* {color:#006644}yes, that’s all clear in principle, but in practice making it clear in the Tool will reduce the number of times it is not removed when it should be. There is a lot of text in each SOP, so things do get missed{color}

{{Use the Customer Safety Support block for Riders in this situation, to align with Driver handling, i.e. make interpretation 2 above the universal handling for both riders and drivers not responding.}}

This is a great idea, however, passengers can see the reasoning in the blocking messages, and we would like to keep the current “Please refer to our messages in your Bolt app.“ for better customer experience. Moreover, the Customer Safety Support block helps the CS and the Risk teams to collect data on drivers who don’t get back to us, and therefore, understand which driver is ready to be unblocked, as their earnings may depend on our platform. With passengers, such demand is irrelevant, so actioning this point may not be feasible.

* {color:#006644}OK I understand the principle, thanks. If this “exception to the normal process” (i.e. remove the investigation block at the end of the investigation) cannot be changed, then can we please change the text on the Tool to say “Leave the investigation block in place”? Also, if this is supposed to be equivalent to a “hard block unless they get back to us”, it will be automatically lifted after 7 years if I understand correctly, so they could in theory go back to using the account, but perhaps that’s not a significant risk.{color}

Other issues with wording include “High Priority Case” for scheduled blocks for Safety cases, and “Customer Safety Support” block only for drivers once the Safety case has been closed. Perhaps there could be separate block types for Safety Investigation Block, Safety Scheduled (or Consequential) Block, and Safety Pending Block or similar instead of Customer Safety Support block (Pending would align with the “Waiting for customer response” status in Beehive)

* {color:#006644}Have you been able to consider this suggested improvement in Admin? Even if we don’t change the procedures or block types at all, changing the wording would be a great help, noting that we are asking agents to write “CS Safety” on the Admin comment when they open the investigation, but to NOT place a Customer Safety Support/”CSS”/”CS Safety” block.{color}

“Please refer to our messages in your Bolt app.“ 

* {color:#006644}Your comment above reminded me to ask about this as well. Yes, I totally agree, this message should be what is sent, and nothing else for any reason. That being the case, why are we giving agents the option to write anything they want, and not making it an a simple drop-down? It could have 1. “Please refer to our messages in your Bolt app.“, 2. “Your account has been suspended. Please refer to our messages in your Bolt app.“, and 3. “Other” which opens a text box, if someone does need to use this method to send other messages.{color}
* {color:#006644}I found it very strange when the option to add messages to drivers was added recently, opening up the possibility of agents sending ECs or other messages to drivers. To me, messages should be sent through Beehive, not through Admin. We make it very, very clear during training and coaching that only the official message can be sent, but it still happens because there’s a box there asking the agent to send the “blocking reason”…{color}

{color:#006644}Please do message me if I’m unclear, I know I can get a bit wordy!{color}

Regards,

Mark

